  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The ranking shows federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date totaling $697B. The Department of Defense obligated $360B, making the Department of Defense the largest contracting agency in dollar terms. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $79.6B, the Department of Homeland Security obligated $69.1B, and the Department of Energy obligated $52.7B. General Services Administration obligated $27B, the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $22.5B, and National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $15.4B. The Department of Transportation obligated $13.3B, the Department of Agriculture obligated $10.1B, and the Department of Justice obligated $9.7B. These figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date. Use the list when sizing pursuit strategies, prioritizing agency business development, or benchmarking capture plans against peer agency spending levels.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top federal contract spenders?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads with $360B in obligations. The Department of Veterans Affairs follows with $79.6B, and the Department of Homeland Security follows with $69.1B. The Department of Energy, General Services Administration, and Department of Health and Human Services round out the mid-range spenders with $52.7B, $27B, and $22.5B respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are contract obligations among agencies?",
        "body": "The total of federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date is $697B. The Department of Defense accounts for $360B of that total. Nine other agencies in the top ten report obligations from $79.6B down to $9.7B. These figures are partial fiscal year totals and represent reported contract obligations to date."
      }
    ]
  },
